opennyai_summary.facts:
Vide this appeal, the appellant has challenged the order dated 06.09.2018 of Ld. Adjudicating Officer/Additional District Magistrate (New Delhi), Delhi ("ADM" in short) directing the appellants no. 1 & 2 (partner of the supplier firm and the firm) to pay a consolidated penalty of Rs.30,000/
and appellants no.3 & 4 (director & nominee, respectively of manufacturing company) to pay a consolidated penalty of Rs.60,000/ under Section 52 of the Food Safety and Standard Act, 2006 ("FSS Act" in short) for violation of Sections 26(2)(ii), 23(1) & 27(3)(c) read with Section 3(1)(zf)(c)(i) of FSS Act and Regulation 2.2.2.2(f)(i) of The Food Safety and Standards(Packaging and Labelling) Regulations, 2011 ("FSS(P&L) Regulations" in short) for selling misbranded food.
The Food Business Operator did not request to send the fourth part of the sample for analysis from NABL accredited laboratory under Rule 2.4.5 of The Food Safety and Standards Rules,2011(FSSR).
Hence , Regulation 2.2.2.2(f) Mr.Sandeep Sudhan & Ors. vs State, FSO Page No.4 of 15 :: 5 ::
(i) of FSS(P&L) Regulations was not applicable to the sample food article.
The only requirement for the standardized food article/ mixed fruit jam is to give the names or ingredients as per 2.2.2.2(b) of FSS(P&L) Regulations;
(ii) Section 32 FSS Act mandates that prosecution should not be initiated for minor defects like the labelling defect in the instant case.
Despite being informed that the alleged violation, though not made out, has been rectified, the Ld.ADM proceeded with prosecution and imposed the penalty;
(iii) the Ld.ADM failed to appreciate that defence under Section 80(B)(2)(a)(d)(e)
FSS Act was available to the appellants no.1 & 2 in view of the fact that the sample food article was sold in the same condition as purchased from the manufacturer company M/s Vidya Sagar Food Pvt. Ltd.vide valid invoices; and it was purchased by the FSO during inspection on 29.05.2017 in the same sealed condition, in which it was purchased by the FBO/ M/s The Leela Palace.
(iv) the Ld. ADM failed to appreciate that Delhi Food Laboratory in which the sample was analyzed and analytical report was given, is not a legally competent lab to analyze the sample food article as per Section 43 FSS Act; it is neither NABL Accredited nor notified in Official Gazette as per provisions Section 3(1)(p) FSS Act;
(v) report of Food Analyst is not admissible as per provisions of FSS Act unless proved by the Food Analyst.
Because it is not conclusive (as per Evidence Act) unlike under PFA Act 1954;
(vi) penalty has been imposed for violation of Regulations No. 2.2.2.3 & 2.2.2.10 of FSS(P&L) Regulations although, neither was violation of said provisions reported in Food Analyst's Report nor was the complaint filed for violation of the same;
